lesson writing-growing-a-sentence v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=3 subject=writing levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Growing a sentence"
  goal "Make a short sentence longer by answering when, where and why, and join two short sentences into one."

  look {
    scene 25x18 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        whale w spout=1 facing=1
        expander e kernel="The whale sang." asks=["when", "where"] answers=["at dawn", "under the ship"]
      }
    }
    say """
      A short sentence can grow. Ask it questions: when did the whale sing, where, why? Each answer
      adds to the sentence. A when at the start is followed by a comma. The short sentence is still
      there in the middle of the long one.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked writing.which-words-tell s=0 q=1 level=medium
    }
    practice writing.which-words-tell count=1 seed=1
    practice writing.which-question-answered count=2 seed=2 hard-seed=7
    practice writing.where-comma-goes count=2 seed=3 hard-seed=7
    practice writing.combine-sentences count=1 seed=2
    practice writing.combine-three count=1 hard-count=0 seed=4
    level hard {
      practice writing.grow-for-reader count=1 seed=1
    }
    show writing.grow-it t="The ship rocked."
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show writing.grow-for-reader k=2 easy-k=0
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show writing.grow-to-ten t="The gull landed."
  }
  remember {
    say "Ask when, where and why. Each answer grows the sentence."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Growing a short sentence and joining two short ones are the two sentence exercises with the
    best evidence behind them for children of this age. Most questions are marked by the machine;
    the grown sentences are yours to read. Check that each is still one sentence, with one full
    stop. In the two-star question the reader decides which question to answer, so ask the child
    who the reader is before they choose. The ten-word sentence in the three-star question has many
    right answers, and counting the words together is part of the fun.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the words that tell when are found once in the teacher's pen before where and who, the grown words answer where or when, the comma follows a short opener, the sentences to combine are as written, the sentence to grow is as written, the two-star's reader needs a where, and the three-star is as written."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words that tell are as written, the grown words answer why, the comma follows a long opener (as the sun set, in the middle of the night), the sentences to combine are as written and the three-in-one makes way for a second reader to grow for, the sentence to grow is as written, and the two-star and three-star are as written."
  }
}
